(include "./bar.yuck")

; Window for the bar. One instance per connected monitor, opened by
; scripts/sync-bars as `bar-<monitor name>`. `screen` is populated from the
; --screen flag and threaded down so each bar can report its own monitor
; (eww variables are global, so the widgets index per-monitor maps by name).
(defwindow bar [screen]
	:monitor screen
	:geometry (geometry
		:x "0%"
		:y "9px"
		:width "98%"
		:height "30px"
		:anchor "top center"
	)
	:stacking "fg"
	:reserve (struts :distance "40px" :side "top")
	:windowtype "dock"
	:wm-ignore false
	:exclusive true
	:focusable false

	(bar :screen screen)
)

; Popups for the volume and brightness sliders, one window per control per
; monitor (`popup-<monitor>-<kind>`), opened by CLICKING the bar icon and
; dismissed by scripts/close-control-popup when the pointer leaves both the icon
; and the popup. Hover does not open anything -- it only records where the
; pointer is, which is the whole reason pop_icon exists.
;
; They are windows rather than revealers in the bar because a revealer
; participates in layout: opening one widened the right-hand section, which
; shoved the centred window title sideways on every hover. Nothing inside the
; bar can avoid that, which is why this is a window and not a widget change.
;
; Two definitions rather than one taking a `kind` argument, so there is no
; invisible twin scale -- eww evaluates a widget's attributes whether or not it
; is visible, so a hidden scale's :onchange still fires when the window opens.
;
; `:exclusive false` is the load-bearing one: the bar reserves struts, and a
; popup that did the same would carve a strip out of the usable screen for as
; long as it was open, shoving tiled windows around on every hover.
;
; The x offsets are constants because eww anchors windows to the *monitor* and
; nothing can ask where a widget actually landed -- `eww debug` prints the scope
; graph, not geometry. They work because the right section is `:halign "end"`,
; so everything packs from the right edge and each icon sits a stable distance
; from it no matter how wide the systray has grown.
;
; Each popup gets its own offset. Brightness is drawn to the *left* of volume,
; so hiding it on a monitor with no backlight moves only what is further left
; still -- volume's distance from the right edge is identical on every bar. An
; earlier version shared one offset, on the belief that hiding brightness would
; shift volume; that had the packing direction backwards, and the shared value
; sat between the two icons rather than under either.
;
; MEASURED, not estimated. `:x` is the distance from the monitor's right edge to
; the window's RIGHT edge -- confirmed by opening a popup at x=248 on a 1600-wide
; output and reading `hyprctl layers`, which put it at x=1308 with w=44
; (1600 - 248 - 44). So centring a 44px window on an icon whose centre is C is
; x = monitor_width - (C + 22).
;
; Icon centres came from logging `hyprctl cursorpos` against pop_icon while the
; pointer was swept over them: brightness 1344, volume 1362 on eDP-1 (logical
; 1600 wide -- it runs at scale 1.20, so 1920 physical is the WRONG number to
; work in, and an earlier set of offsets was wrong partly for that reason).
; BOTH outputs are fractionally scaled now -- the external is 3840x2160 at scale
; 1.5, so 2560 is ITS logical width -- so there is no output left where the
; physical number is safe to reach for. Note a *warped* cursor is no use here:
; hyprctl dispatch movecursor teleports without the crossing GTK needs, so an
; automated sweep reports nothing at all.
;
; Volume is one value for two monitors that disagree by 10px, because the icons
; sit a fixed distance from the BAR's right edge while the bar itself is inset by
; 1% of the monitor (16px on eDP-1, 26px on the 2560-wide external). eDP-1 alone
; wants 216 and the external alone wants 226; 221 splits it, leaving ~5px of error
; against a 17px icon. What the inset tracks is LOGICAL width -- which is the
; physical width DIVIDED BY the scale, so the scale is an input to it, not an
; irrelevance. The external survived a move to 4K at scale 1.5 only because that
; PRODUCT is unchanged at 2560; scale 2.0 on the same panel would give 1920
; logical and a 19px inset. Re-measured after that move: `hyprctl layers` puts
; its bar at x=1626, which is 26 into a monitor whose own origin is x=1600 (the
; :x convention above is monitor-relative; on eDP-1 the two coincide because it
; sits at 0x0). Identify this monitor by that logical width, not by a connector.
; Brightness only ever opens where there is a backlight, so 234 is exact.
;
; `:y` is measured from the bottom of the bar's EXCLUSIVE ZONE, not from the top
; of the screen -- the bar reserves a 40px strut, so y=39 rendered at y=78 and
; left a 39px dead strip between bar and popup. That strip, not any debounce
; length, is why a slow crossing kept dismissing the popup: the pointer was over
; neither window for the whole traverse. y=0 puts the popup flush against the bar.
;
; The two geometries are duplicated rather than shared because `geometry` is a
; struct, not a widget, so a defwidget cannot return one. Keep y and the size in
; step by hand; only x is meant to differ.
(defwindow volume-popup [screen]
	:monitor screen
	:geometry (geometry
		:x "221px"
		:y "0px"
		:width "44px"
		:height "170px"
		:anchor "top right"
	)
	:stacking "overlay"
	:exclusive false
	:focusable false

	(volume-popup-body :screen screen)
)

(defwindow brightness-popup [screen]
	:monitor screen
	:geometry (geometry
		:x "234px"
		:y "0px"
		:width "44px"
		:height "170px"
		:anchor "top right"
	)
	:stacking "overlay"
	:exclusive false
	:focusable false

	(brightness-popup-body :screen screen)
)
